course: dictionary
lesson: 13551
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word improper is a good example. Its first part, im-, means not, and proper means right or suitable, so improper means not right or not suitable.
explain: «It was improper to shout in the library.» Shouting is not suitable for a library, so improper means not polite for the situation. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «It was not polite to shout in the library.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Maths uses improper in a special way. «7/4 is an improper fraction.» This does not mean the fraction is wrong. It means the top number is bigger than the bottom number. Always look at the clues around a word to find its meaning in each subject.
fact: Improper means not suitable or not polite for the situation.
fact: Improper can also mean not correct or not following the rules, as in «improper use of tools».
fact: In maths, an improper fraction has a top number equal to or bigger than its bottom number, like 7/4.
fact: An improper fraction can be changed into a mixed number: 7/4 = 1 3/4.
fact: Improper use of tools can cause accidents, so always follow the safety rules.
exercise: «It was improper to talk loudly during the film.» What does improper mean here? | Brand new | Very funny | Easy to read | *Not polite for the situation
exercise: In maths, which of these is an improper fraction? | 3/4 | 2/5 | 1/2 | *7/4
exercise: «Ben used the saw in an improper way and cut his finger.» What does improper suggest about how the saw was used? | Happily | *Not correct, against the safety rules | Carefully and slowly | With a new blade
test: «The teacher said Kai's remark was improper, so he said sorry.» What does improper mean? | Shiny | Very long | Polite and kind | *Not suitable or polite for the situation
test: Which fraction is improper, because its top number is equal to or bigger than its bottom number? | 3/8 | 1/4 | *5/5 | 2/3
exam: In «an improper remark», what does improper mean? | Hidden under the bed | Very long | *Not polite or suitable for the situation | Kind and helpful
